    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.lexicographic_product - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.lexicographic_product.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.lexicographic_product()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Compute the lexicographic product (composition) of this graph with another.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.lexicographic_product()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Compute the lexicographic product (composition) of this graph with another.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.lexicographic_product()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Compute the lexicographic product (composition) of this graph with another.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCompute the lexicographic product (composition) of this graph with another.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L51841-L51937){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.lexicographic_product(other)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Lexicographic product](../assets/graphs/lexicographic-product.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"G is a single edge between departments sales (S) and ops (O), accent; H is a team, an edge between lead (L) and rep (R), teal. The lexicographic product puts a copy of the team in each department (the shaded groups, L and R in each): the teal edges copy H inside each copy, and because sales and ops are linked, four accent edges join every member of one team to every member of the other. The result is a complete graph on 4 nodes with 6 edges.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nThe lexicographic product $G[H]$ replaces every node of this graph by a copy of `other`,\nand joins *every* node of one copy to every node of another copy whenever the two\noriginal nodes are adjacent. Formally, `(g, h)` and `(g', h')` are joined if `g`-`g'` is\nan edge, or if `g = g'` and `h`-`h'` is an edge of `other`. It is the natural model of\na network of modules: departments (this graph) staffed by teams (`other`), where linked\ndepartments have all-to-all contact between their members. Unlike the other products,\nit is not commutative.\n\nBoth graphs must be directed or both undirected. Every product edge comes from one\nfactor edge, whose weight and attributes it keeps.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[other]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The graph substituted for each node. Neither graph is modified.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A new graph with `n * m` nodes, numbered and labeled as in `cartesian_product()`.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If one graph is directed and the other is not.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nAn undirected simple product has $|E_G| m^2 + n |E_H|$ edges. Results match NetworkX's\n`lexicographic_product`.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~strong_product`) \n\n:   Combine the Cartesian and tensor products.\n\n[](`~rooted_product`) \n\n:   Hang a copy of another graph from each node.\n\n[](`~full_join`) \n\n:   Combine two graphs and join every pair across them.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThe lexicographic product puts a copy of the second graph at every node of the first, and\njoins every node of two copies whose nodes are joined in the first. Two linked\ndepartments, each a team of a lead and a rep, give a complete graph on four nodes:\n\n::: {#bc8208fb .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ndepartments = ec.Graph().add_nodes([\"sales\", \"ops\"])\ndepartments.add_edge(\"sales\", \"ops\")\nteam = ec.Graph().add_nodes([\"lead\", \"rep\"]).add_edge(\"lead\", \"rep\")\n\norg = departments.lexicographic_product(team)\norg.count_edges(), org.is_isomorphic(ec.Graph().add_full_graph(4))\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(6, True)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nWith no edge inside each team, only the four links across the departments remain:\n\n::: {#3476454c .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nsolo = ec.Graph().add_nodes([\"lead\", \"rep\"])\ndepartments.lexicographic_product(solo).count_edges()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n4\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Three teams in a row\n\nThree departments in a line, each with a team of three who all work together. Each team\nis a triangle, and everyone in a team is joined to everyone in the teams next to it. A\nmember of ops, in the middle, works with 2 teammates and the 6 people on either side:\n\n::: {#af28a291 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nline = ec.Graph().add_nodes([\"sales\", \"ops\", \"dev\"])\nline.add_edges([(\"sales\", \"ops\"), (\"ops\", \"dev\")])\ntrio = ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(3)\n\nstaff = line.lexicographic_product(trio)\npeople = staff.get_node_df()\ndept = {\n    n: label.strip(\"(\").split(\",\")[0]\n    for n, label in zip(people[\"id\"], people[\"label\"])\n}\nstaff.set_node_attrs(\"dept\", dept)\n\nstaff.get_degree(\"(sales, 1)\"), staff.get_degree(\"(ops, 1)\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(5, 8)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#6beb6331 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nstaff.plot(color_by=\"dept\", label_by=\"label\", seed=1)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div 